===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}} {{term-label|en|originally|US|informal}}

# {{lb|en|countable|chiefly|in the plural}} {{singular of|en|shenanigans}}: a [[deceitful]] [[confidence trick]]; also, an [[act#Noun|act]] of [[mischief#Noun|mischief]]; a [[prank#Noun|prank]], a [[trick#Noun|trick]]; an act of [[mischievous]] [[play#Noun|play]], especially by [[child#Noun|children]].
#: {{ux|en|I spotted his next '''shenanigan'''—saw it coming—and so avoided being fooled.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=18 August 1854|title=First performance of the original Dolly Wags at Cayoteville|editor=E. R. Budd|journal=w:The Nevada Journal|location=Nevada City, Calif.|publisher=Budd & Skelton|volume=4|issue=16 (number 173 overall)|page=1|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-nevada-journal-shenanigan/173109582/|column=5|oclc=210694656|passage=He then would state some of the principles of the party; First of all he would have them know it was on the square! and why was it on the square? Why? Because it didn't intend to "come the '''shenanigan'''"—that was it!}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=31 December 1854|title=Questions and answers|journal=The Wide West|location=San Francisco, Calif.|publisher=Bonestell & Williston|volume=I|issue=42|page=&#91;2&#93;|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/widewest1857bone/page/n135/mode/1up|column=1|oclc=10859476|passage={{smallcaps|Abstrusus}}–In your answers to correspondence last week is given a definition to which I must except: An Irish word which you write ‘'''shenanigan'''’ is defined as being synonymous with our English word ‘humbug,’ thus depriving the natives of ‘the Breast-pin of the Ocean’ of the credit of great shrewdness in the use of the word.|brackets=on}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=31 August 1855|title=Turn out Americans!!|editor=[[w:Edwin G. Waite|E[dwin] G Waite]]|journal=w:The Nevada Journal|location=Nevada City, Calif.|publisher=N. P. Brown & Co.|volume=5|issue=18 (number 277 overall)|page=2|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-nevada-journal-shenanigan/173110101/|column=2|oclc=210694656|passage=We have a host of eagle-eyed natives, who will stand by the polls, and see that no '''shenanigan''' is played.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992|author=Celia Morris|chapter=7|title=Storming the Statehouse: Running for Governor with Ann Richards and {{w|Dianne Feinstein}}|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Charles Scribner's Sons|Charles Scribner’s Sons]]; Don Mills, Ont.: [[w:Macmillan Inc.|Maxwell Macmillan Canada]]|page=148|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/stormingstatehou00morr/page/148/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-684-19328-1|passage=A longtime friend of [[w:Ann Richards|{{quote-gloss|Ann}} Richards]] and co-conspirator in many a '''shenanigan''' and act of political skulduggery, she {{quote-gloss|{{w|Liz Carpenter}}}} had decided early in the fall to act on her conviction that something important had gone out of politics.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|dated|rare}} [[dishonest|Dishonest]], [[underhanded]], or [[unscrupulous]] [[activity|activities]] or [[behavior|behaviour]]; [[skulduggery]], [[trickery]]; also, mischievous behaviour or play; [[high jinks]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1855|month=September|title=Mr. Soap’s Yachting Excursion|magazine=Yankee-notions|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=T. W. Strong,{{nb...|98 Nassau-st.}}|volume=IV|issue=9|page=280|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=7JFRAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA280|column=1|oclc=32423418|passage=One of Professor {{quote-gloss|John}} Moon's most astonishing "experiments," consisted of holding a watch suspended from a short chain at arm's length, and allowing anyone in the pit to pull pistol and "blaze away" at the word "fire," whereupon the watch would most unconscionably disappear. An individual who had attended several evenings and witnessed the "experiment," suspected, in the classic language of the times, that there was something of "'''shenanigan'''" in it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=['''1877''']|author=Cha[rle]s H. Sparks|chapter=Political History|title=History of Winneshiek County, with Biographical Sketches of Its Eminent Men|location=Decorah, Iowa|publisher=Jas. Alex. Leonard|page=31|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/historyofwinnesh01spar/page/31/mode/1up|oclc=995345986|passage=There are stories still told how money was used and promised, but from the best knowledge I can acquire, I think this is not true. If sharp practice was played, and "'''shenanigan'''" was used, we, to-day, looking back upon those times, cannot say that evil has come of it.}}

